Example
Hackers used LinkedIn to grab contact information from employees at Sony and targeted them with an email phishing campaign. They got away with over 100 terabytes of data.
Suspicious Sender Email Address
The email may look legitimate but contains a slightly altered domain.
Example:
Fake: support-linkedin@mail-security.com
Real: @linkedin.com
This difference suggests the message may not be from the real organization.
2. Suspicious Links or URLs
Phishing emails often include links that resemble the real website but lead to malicious pages.
Example:
Fake link: linkedin-security-update.com
Real link: linkedin.com
These fake links are designed to steal login credentials.
3. Urgent or Threatening Language
Attackers try to create panic so the victim acts quickly without verifying the message.
Common phrases include:
“Your account will be suspended”
“Immediate verification required”
“Urgent security alert”
4. Request for Sensitive Information
Legitimate companies rarely ask for sensitive information through email. Phishing messages may request:
-Passwords
-Bank details
-Verification codes
-Personal identification information
5. Unexpected Attachments
Some phishing emails contain attachments that may install malware when opened.
Impact of a Phishing Attack
1. Financial Loss
If banking or payment details are stolen, victims may lose money from their accounts.
2. Unauthorized Account Access
Attackers may gain control of the victim’s online accounts such as email or social media.
3. Spread of Further Attacks
Once attackers control an account, they may send phishing messages to the victim’s contacts.
4. Damage to Reputation
If a professional account such as LinkedIn is compromised, it may harm the user’s professional reputation or relationships.
